\section{Introduction} 
%\section[short_title]{Introduction} 
% \subsection{Topic}
% \frame{\frametitle{Topic}
% Each frame should have a title.
% }
%\subsection{Topic \& Motivation}
\frame{\frametitle{Topic}

An analysis of the impact of macroeconomic and bank-specific factors on Non-Performing Loans (NPLs)  in the EU-banking sector using an extensive,
 granular dataset on major European banks gathered by the European Banking Authority (EBA) in the context of the EU-wide Transparency Exercise for the period 2014-2019.
 
}


%\cite{BergerA.N.&DeYoungR.2001}
%\cite{BergerA.N.DeYoung.2000}
%\cite{Viral.2006}
%\frame{\frametitle{NPL Determinants I - Relevance \cite{EBA6}  \cite{BergerA.N.&DeYoungR.2001} \cite{Quagliariello}}
\frame{\frametitle{ Motivation I \cite{EBA} \cite{EBA6} \cite{guideline} \cite{BernankeBS.1981}}
\begin{itemize}
\item NPLs are a main indicator of bank's asset quality.
\item The term NPL usually refers to loans that are over 90 days past-due or unlikely to be repaid in full

          %\item NPL is a main indicator of bank's asset quality.

 %\item Important effects of financial intermediation instability on the real economy.
\item Important effects of financial intermediation on the real economy.

      \item Understanding the determinants of Non-Performing Loans (NPL) is a very relevant topic for economic policy in Europe.  In the last years, non-performing loans have been a major concern for supervisor,  policymakers and market participants in the EU.
      
      \item Increase importance of understanding  NPL determinants in  the inmediate  pre-COVID period and their transmission channels.


%             \item A more granular analysis of each loan type at the disaggregate level would contribute to a further
% understanding of the determinants of NPL


      % \item In the aftermath of the financial crisis, non-performing loans (NPLs) have been a major concern for supervisor,  policymakers and market participants in the EU.

%
%       \item A more granular analysis of each loan type at the disaggregate level would improve the
% understanding of determinants of NPL significantly.
%
%
%       \item  The impact of softer "political" factors
% like  supervisory mechanisms or quality of the legal system needs to be
% addressed in detail. Important implications for policy makers in
% the EU, where membership negotiations take place.
% 
%     \item  The impact of geographical loan diversification on bank's assets quality.



     \item Implications for prudent bank stress testing, EU policy making, recommendations on NPL strategies and design of Early Warning Indicators (EWI), contributing to a more effective microprudential supervision of  banks in Europe.

      
      
\end{itemize}

}


\frame{\frametitle{Motivation II - Research on NPL Determinants \& Bank-Level Data \cite{FSI} \cite{Cerutti}}
 %\cite{Platon}
 \begin{itemize}
          
        \item  Many authors  indicate the need of a more granular analysis of NPL drivers at bank-level.

      \item Challenges in the NPL literature when using individual bank data:\\
         \begin{itemize}


         \item Data availability is limited due to data protection regulation and banking law.
         
         \item Private platforms like Dealscan, Datastream, Bankscope or Debtwire suffers from a representativeness bias and data quality issues.
         \item Banking supervisory data is usually provided in a domestically controlled cross-border consolidation basis.
         
         \item The use of macroeconomic data  based on  the bank's country of origin does not totally account  for cross-border portfolio diversification in the banking sector when analysing its impact on non-performing loans 
         
         \item Divergences concerning the definition of non-performing loans and the method of compilation of the data across countries.
        \end{itemize}
        
        \item Example of the potential benefits from using the data of the EBA's EU-wide Transparency Exercise for supervisory and academic purposes with respect to these challenges
        
         \item  The use of the data of the EBA's EU-wide Transparency Exercise is also quite a novelty in the  academic research.
      %   
      % \item  The EBA's EU-wide Transparency Exercise  provided  extensive, public and granular portfolio-by-portfolio supervisory  data on major European banks since 2014  using an harmonised the definition of NPLs, allowing a cross-country comparability of NPLS across European banks,
      %  \item  The use of data published as part of the EBA transparency exercise  to analyse the determinants of NPLs is quite a novelty.
      
      
      
           
            
            %for instance, accounting for disaggregated loan portfolio characteristics.
            
            %in the NPL literature to a further understanding of the determinants of NPL


       \end{itemize}
 
 }

\section{EBA \& SSM}
\frame{\frametitle{The EBA's EU-wide Transparency Exercise  \cite{EBA6} \cite{guideline}}

\begin{itemize}


\item The European Banking Authority (EBA) is an independent EU Authority established on 1 January 2011 as part of the European System of Financial Supervision (ESFS) to ensure effective and consistent prudential regulation and supervision across the European banking sector.

% \item  With the EU-wide transparency exercise, the EBA has been disclosing detailed bank-by-bank data on  an annual basis since 2016, with the objective of providing the general public and market participants with updated information on the financial conditions of EU banks at the highest level of consolidation.

\item  With the EU-wide transparency exercise, the EBA has been disclosing data on  an annual basis since 2016, which:
   
   \begin{itemize}
   
    \item  Provides the general public and market participants with updated (quaterly/semesterly) information on the financial conditions of EU banks at the highest level of consolidation

   \item Discloses an extensive, and granular portfolio-by-portfolio supervisory  data on major European banks -exposures are broken down by banks, asset classes, default status, ect..-. representing approximately between 80\% and 85\% of banking assets in the European Union.
   
   \item Uses an harmonised definition of NPLs across European countries.
   
   \item Includes data on original exposures up to the 10 largest countries of counterparty at bank level
    \item Provides information regarding membership status under the Single Supervisory Mechanism (SSM)
   
    \end{itemize}

% \item  The previous mentioned characteristics in terms of granurality and high standards of data quality provided an adequate framework to study the determinants of the NPLs at bank-level .
%\cite{Kerbl}\cite{Turk}
 
 
 \end{itemize}
}


