\section{Empirical Approach} 
% \frame{\frametitle{Title6}
% Each frame should have a title.
% }
%\subsection{Linear \& Dynamic Panel Regressions}
\frame{\frametitle{Linear  \& GMM Panel Models \cite{Baltagi.2005} \cite{Arellano.Bover.1995}}

\begin{itemize}
\item Static Models (Random Effects/Fixed Effects):\\

\[
y_{it} = \alpha  + X'_{it}\beta + u_{it} \quad i=1,..., N;\enspace t=1,..., T
\]
\\

%Following methods are used:
% \begin{itemize}
% \item Pooling
% \item Random Effects 
% \item Fixed Effects
% \end{itemize}

\item Dynamic Models-GMM for short panel data (Arellano-Bover Estimators):\\

\[
y_{it} = \delta y_{i,t-1}  + X'_{it}\beta + u_{it} \quad i=1,..., N;\enspace t=1,..., T
\]
\\	


\item $y_{it}$ represents the non-performing ratio for bank $i$ in year $t$, \alpha$ is a scalar, $\beta$ is $K \,\times\, 1$ and $X_{it}$ is the \textit{it}th observation on $K$ explanatory variables. The $K$ explanatory variables consist of bank-specific and macroeconomic variables. $\delta$ is a scalar and  $y_{i,t-1}$  is the lagged dependent variable (NPL\_ratio).

\item $ u_{it}$ follows a one-way component model 

\[
u_{it} = \mu_{i} + \upsilon_{it}
\]
\\

where $\mu_{i}$ denotes the unobservable  individual bank-specific effects and $\upsilon_{it}$ denotes the remainder disturbance.

\item $\mu_{i}\;\sim$IID$(0,\,\sigma^2_{\mu})$ and $\upsilon_{it}\;\sim$IID$(0,\,\sigma^2_{\upsilon})$ and the error disturbances $\upsilon_{it}$ are assumed to be mutually uncorrelated $\mathbb{E}(\upsilon_{it}\, \upsilon_{is})= 0\,\, \forall s \neq t$ and not correlated with the individual effects $\mathbb{E}(\mu_{i}\, \upsilon_{it})= 0$.

\end{itemize}
 
% Following methods are used:
% \begin{itemize}
% \item Difference GMM Estimator
% \item System GMM Estimator
% 
% \end{itemize}




}


%\subsection{Quantil Regression}



\frame{\frametitle{Pooled Quantil Regression \cite{Koenker.2002} \cite{Koenker.2005}}

\begin{itemize}

\item Quantile regression consider the relationship between the explanatory variables and the dependent variable using the conditional quantile function $Q_{\tau}(y|x)$.

\item Quantil regression shows more robutness in samples with many outliers than OLS and it is semiparametric, i.e. it does not make assumptions about the parametric distribution of the errors.

\item Quantile regression methods also  allows the  analysis of the impact of a set of explanatory variables on the entire conditional distribution of the dependent variable. 

%$Q_{y} (\tau|\enspace x)$ represents the conditional $\tau$-quantile of $y$ given ($x$). 

\item The quantile $\tau \in (0,1)$ determines the point of $y$ in which the data is split into proportions under $\tau$ and above $1-\tau$. Quantile regression seeks to minimize  the errors $\epsilon_{i}$ that gives asymmetric penalties $(1-\tau)\lvert \epsilon_{i}\rvert$ for overprediction and $\tau\lvert \epsilon_{i}\rvert$  for underprediction.

%\item The optimization problem in the quantile regression is dependent on the specific quantile in evaluation. 
% \item The optimization problem in the quantile regression is dependent on the specific quantile in evaluation. In this context, more negative errors suffer higher penalties for higher quantiles while more positive errors are more penalized for lower quantiles. In other words, $Q_{y} (\tau|\enspace x)$ represents the conditional $\tau$-quantile of $y$ given ($x$). The quantile $\tau \in (0,1)$ determines the point of $y$ in which the data is split into proportions under $\tau$ and above $1-\tau$, that is $F(y_{\tau}=\tau$ and $y_{\tau}=F^{-1}(\tau)$.  Quantile regression seeks to minimize  the errors $\epsilon_{i}$ that gives asymmetric penalties $(1-\tau)\lvert \epsilon_{i}\rvert$ for overprediction and $\tau\lvert \epsilon_{i}\rvert$  for underprediction.

\item The quantile regression estimator in quantile $\tau$ is the one that minimizes the following objective function:
\[
Q(\beta_{\tau})  \,=\,  \sum_{y_{i} \geq x'_{i}\beta}^n \, \tau \lvert y_{i}-x'_{i}\beta_{\tau}\rvert \, +\,+ \sum_{y_{i} < x'_{i}\beta}^n \, (1-\tau) \lvert y_{i}-x'_{i}\beta_{\tau}\rvert

\]
\\
\item The estimator have been showed to be asymptotically normal and bootstrap standard errors are  often used instead of traditional standard errors.

\end{itemize}

}

\frame{\frametitle{Fixed Effects Panel Quantil Regression (Penalized FE Method) \cite{Koenker.2004} \cite{Koenker.2017}}

\begin{itemize}

\item The penalized least squares interpretation offers the possibility of perform quantile regression models accounting for the existence of individual effects in  panel data.

\item In the Penalized FE Method, the following penalized estimator $(\hat{\alpha}(\lambda),\hat{\beta}(\lambda,\tau)$ is considered
 
\[
\min_{(\alpha,\beta)} \enspace \dfrac{1}{nT} \sum_{k=1}^q \sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{t=1}^T \omega_{k} \rho_{\tau}(y_{it}-\alpha_{i}-X'_{it}\beta(\tau_{k}))+ \lambda \sum_{i=1}^n \lvert\alpha_{i}\rvert
\]
\\

where $\omega_{1},...,\omega_{q}$ are nonnegative weigths and $\lambda \geq 0$ is the penalty level.

 \item The idea in this model is to estimate several $\tau$ simultaneously and restrict the individual effect coefficients  $\alpha_{i}$ to be the same for all quantiles and which  values shrinks them toward zero  as  the penalty level $\lambda$ increases. Therefore, the parameter $\lambda$ allows  a calibration of the impact of the penalty.
% 
\item In general, when $\lambda \to 0$ the model is similar to a (weigthed) dummy-variable quantile regression and when $\lambda \to \infty$ the model is more like a (weigthed) cross-section quantile regression, i.e, the lower the value of $\lambda$, the larger the control for fixed effects in the model.

\item The penaly allows a "control" over the many fixed effects coefficients by not letting them reach unrealistic large numbers.



\end{itemize}
}


% \frame{\frametitle{Fixed Effects Panel Quantil Regression (Penalized FE Method) I \cite{Koenker.2004}}
% 
% \begin{itemize}
% 
% \item The penalized least squares interpretation offers the possibility of perform quantile regression models accounting for the existence of individual effects in  panel data. 
% 
% \item In a panel Quantile regression, there is pairs of dependent  and explanatory variables $(y_{it},x_{it})$ with $i=1,..., N;\enspace t=1,..., T$, where $i$ denoting  the $ith$ individual and $t$ denoting time. Furthermore, there also are  unobservable individual-specific variables $\mu_{i}$ with $i=1,..., N$, representing individual characteristics.
% 
%  \item Formally,
%  
% \[
% y_{it} \, =\, \alpha_{i} (\mu_{i}) \, + \, X'_{it}\beta(\tau) \,+\, u_{it}(\tau) 
% \]
% \\
% 
% where $u_{it}(\tau)$ has a zero conditional $\tau$-quantile given $(x_{it},\mu_{i})$.Under the standard assumption, the conditional quantile restriction is equivalent to 
% 
% \[
% P(u_{it}(\tau) \leq 0 |\enspace x_{it},\mu_{i}) \,=\, \tau
% \]
% \\
% 
% \item A panel quantil regression model with individual effects is necessarily nonlinear and therefore individual heterogeneity can not simply be removed by  differencing or transforming the data as it is usually the case in other panel regression models

% 
% \[
% Q_{y_{it}} (\tau|\enspace x_{it},\mu_{i}) \,=\, \alpha_{i} (\mu_{i})  \,+\, X'_{it}\beta(\tau)
% \]
% \\
% %where the individual effect $\alpha_{i}$ does not vary over quantiles (no scalar effect). 
% where $u_{it}(\tau)$ has a zero conditional $\tau$-quantile given $(x_{it},\mu_{i})$.
% 
% Under the standard assumption, the conditional quantile restiction is equivalent to
% 
% \[
% P(u_{it}(\tau) \leq 0 |\enspace x_{it},\mu_{i}) \,=\, \tau
% \]
% \\
% 
% 
% \end{itemize}
% }

% \frame{\frametitle{Fixed Effects Panel Quantil Regression (Penalized FE Method) II \cite{Koenker.2017}}
% \begin{itemize}
% 
% \item In the Penalized FE Method, the following penalized estimator $(\hat{\alpha}(\lambda),\hat{\beta}(\lambda,\tau)$ is considered
%  
% \[
% \min_{(\alpha,\beta)} \enspace \dfrac{1}{nT} \sum_{k=1}^q \sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{t=1}^T \omega_{k} \rho_{\tau}(y_{it}-\alpha_{i}-X'_{it}\beta(\tau_{k}))+ \lambda \sum_{i=1}^n \lvert\alpha_{i}\rvert
% \]
% \\
% 
% where $\omega_{1},...,\omega_{q}$ are nonnegative weigths and $\lambda \geq 0$ is the penalty level.
% 
%  \item The idea in this model is to estimate several $\tau$ simultaneously and restrict the individual effect coefficients  $\alpha_{i}$ to be the same for all quantiles and which  values shrinks them toward zero  as  the penalty level $\lambda$ increases. Therefore, the parameter $\lambda$ allows  a calibration of the impact of the penalty.
% % 
% \item In general, when $\lambda \to 0$ the model is similar to a (weigthed) dummy-variable quantile regression and when $\lambda \to \infty$ the model is more like a (weigthed) cross-section quantile regression, i.e, the lower the value of $\lambda$, the larger the control for fixed effects in the model.
% 
% \item The penaly allows a "control" over the many fixed effects coefficients by not letting them reach unrealistic large numbers.
% 
% % This estimator is considered to deliver results that are between those of a pure dummy-variable  estimator and a pure cross-section estimator. In general, the estimator tend to perform worse in cases where  the fixed effect is expected to have a scalar effect, that is when the fixed effects shows a variation over quantiles \cite{Bache.2011}.
% 
% \end{itemize}
% }
% \item  The quantil regression  in panel data is interested in the conditional quantile of $y_{it}$ given  ($y_{it},\mu_{i}$) and seek to estimate the partial effect of $x_{it}$ on a conditional quantile. The basic model is then
% 
% 
% \[
% y_{it} \, =\, \alpha_{i} (\tau,\mu_{i}) \, + \, X'_{it}\beta(\tau) \,+\, u_{it}(\tau) 
% \]
% \\
% 
% 
% where $u_{it}(\tau)$ has a zero conditional $\tau$-qunatile given $(x_{it},\mu_{i})$. Under the standard assumption, the conditional quantile restiction is equivalent to 
% 
% \[
% P(u_{it}(\tau) \leq 0 |\enspace x_{it},\mu_{i}) \,=\, \tau
% \]
% \\

% \item A panel quantil regression model with individual effects is necessarilly nonlinear and therefore individual heterogeneity can not simply be removed by  differencing or transforming the data as it is usually the case in other panel regression models

% \item The penalized least squares interpretation of the classical random effects estimator offers the possibility of perform quantile regression models accounting for the existence of individual specific effects as is usually the case with panel data. This allows the analysis of panel data under less stringent distributional assumption. This models are also  fit for "short" panel data where a large number of cross-sectional individuals observed over a short amount of periods ($T<<N$). In general, there has been a growing literature on Quantile Regression methods for panel data.
% 
% \item  Formally, the model is general defined  as 
% 
% \[
% Q_{y_{it}} (\tau|\enspace x_{it},\alpha_{i})} \,=\, \alpha_{i} \,+\, X'_{it}\beta(\tau) \quad i=1,..., N;\enspace t=1,..., T
% \]
% \\
% 
% where the individual effect $\alpha_{i}$ does not vary over quantiles (no scalar effect). Furthermore, the following penalized estimator $(\hat{\alpha}(\lambda),\hat{\beta}(\lambda,\tau)$ is considered
% 
% \[
% \min_{(\alpha,\beta)} \enspace \dfrac{1}{nT} \sum_{k=1}^q \sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{t=1}^T \omega_{k} \rho_{\tau}(y_{it}-\alpha_{i}-X'_{it}\beta(\tau_{k}))+ \lambda \sum_{i=1}^n \lvert\alpha_{i}\rvert}
% \]
% \\
% 
% where $\omega_{1},...,\omega_{q}$ are nonnegative weigths and $\lambda \geq 0$ is the penalty level.
% 
% \item The idea in this model is to estimate several $\tau$ simultaneously and restric the individual effect coefficients  $\alpha_{i$ to be the same for all quantiles and which  values shrinks them toward zero  as  the penalty level $\lambda$ increases. Therefore, the parameter $\lambda$ allows  a calibration of the impact of the penalty.
% 
% \item In general, when $\lambda \to 0$ the model is similar to a (weigthed) dummy-variable quantile regression and when $\lambda \to \infty$ the model is more like a (weigthed) cross-section quantile regression. The penaly allows a "control" over the many fixed effects coefficients by not letting them reach unrealistic large numbers.
% 


% \item This estimator is considered to deliver results that are between those of a pure dummy-variable  estimator and a pure cross-section estimator. In general, the estimator tend to perform worse in cases where  the fixed effect is expected to have a scalar effect, that is when the fixed effects shows a variation over quantiles.


%  
% \[
% Q_{y_{it}} (\tau|\enspace x_{it},\mu_{i})} \,=\, \alpha_{i} (\tau,\mu_{i}) \,+\, X'_{it}\beta(\tau)
% \]
% \\
% 
% where $\alpha_{i} (\tau,\mu_{i})$ is some unknown function of $\tau$ and $\mu_{i}$ and $\beta(\tau)$ is a dimensional vector of unknown parameters to be estimated in the model. In a more familiar form, the model can also be written as
% 
% \[
% y_{it} \, =\, \alpha_{i} (\tau,\mu_{i}) \, + \, X'_{it}\beta(\tau) \,+\, u_{it}(\tau) 
% \]
% \\
% 
% where $u_{it}(\tau)$ has a zero conditional $\tau$-qunatile given $(x_{it},\mu_{i})$. Under the standard assumption, the conditional quantile restiction is equivalent to 
% 
% \[
% P(u_{it}(\tau) \leq 0 |\enspace x_{it},\mu_{i}) \,=\, \tau
% \]
% \\
% 
% A panel quantil regression model with individual effects is necessarilly nonlinear and therefore individual heterogeneity can not simply be removed by  differencing or transforming the data as it is usually the case in other panel regression models



