%\section{Conclusion}
\frame{\frametitle{Results I }

\begin{itemize}

\item  In general, macroeconomic drivers  tend to show slightly more explanatory power than bank-specific variables

\item \textbf{Bank-specific Drivers}

    
    \begin{itemize}
    
    \item Bank profitability $\to$ Returns on Risk adjusted Capital (-), Net Interest Margin (+/?)
   % Economics of information \&  risk appetite ->  Bank size (-)
     \item Cost efficiency  $\to$  Cost to Income (?)
     \item Bank capitalization $\to$ CET1 ratio(-), leverage ratio (+/-)
    %\item Lending policy  $\to$  Net Interest Margin (+/?)
  
    %\item
    
    \end{itemize} 


\item \textbf{Macroeconomic drivers}

    \begin{itemize}
    
     \item Business cycle  $\to$ unemployment rates(+), \& GDP growth (+/-),
     \item Terms of trade  $\to$ current account balance (+/?)
    \item Sovereign Debt  $\to$ Gross Public debt (+)
    \item Rule of law:creditor-protection  $\to$  Recovery rates (-) 
    \item Rule of law:creditor \& debtor-protection  $\to$ strength legal system (+/?) 
    \item Corporare Governance \& Supervision  $\to$ SSMY(-)
    \item Price levels  $\to$ inflaction (+)
    
    \end{itemize}
  

 \end{itemize} 
}
   
   
 \frame{\frametitle{Results II }   
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Disaggregated loan aspects}
    \begin{itemize}
    
    \item Exposure types (households \& non-financial corp.) showed similar effects in term of impact direction  (+/-) but differences in intensity and explanatory power, with financial corp. showing the greatest impact direction deviations and the least explanatory power.
    \item Greatest impact on non-financial Corporations followed by households \& financial corporations
    \item Similar marginal changes for each portfolio type
    \item In general, the marginal impact increases along the conditional NPL-distribution for all portfolio types, showing that the intensity of the impact increases  for banks with higher non-performing ratios
   % \item Differences in marginal impact  along the conditional  NPL-distribution.
    
    \end{itemize}
    
\item Positive impact of lagged NPL ratios

\item The effects of bank-specific and macroeconomic drivers increase \textbf{along the conditional NPL-distribution}, i.e., stronger impacts  as the NPL levels in portfolio increase

\item  \textbf{Regulation and Supervision under the Single Supervisory Mechanism}  is significant and  negatively correlated with NPL with similar effects along the conditional NPL distibution

\item   The effect of \textbf{geographic diversification}  are unclear
%for households \& non-financial corporations. No impact for financial corporations.
%When controlling for  macroeconomic drivers,the effects are slighly positive for households. Strong \% non-financial corporations. 
%otherwise negative. Larger impact for non-financial corporations.

\end{itemize}


}

\section{Conclusion}
\frame{\frametitle{Conclusion}
\begin{itemize}

\item Contribute with a more disaggregated  analysis of determinants of NPL  based on bank-portfolio composition, using novel supervisory data provided by EBA showing:


\begin{itemize}
\item	Importance of bank-specific \& regulatory ratios on explaining NPLs in terms of bank-profitability, bank-capitalization, portfolio strategy and risk appetite

\item	Importance of macroeconomic indicators taking geographic composition in portfolio into consideration
 \item A first estimation of the impact of geographic diversification on NPLs
\item	 Relevance of regulatory supervision \& legal aspects on explaining NPLs
\item	Importance of disaggregated loan-portfolios in NPLs research

%: Divergencies in NPLS determinants

 %Divergencies in NPLS determinants in terms of Impact direction and intensity
%split by loan type (financial corp., non-financial corp.,households)
\item	Importance of analysing NPL determinants conditional on NPL levels 
%The greater NPLs are, the greater the impact is.

\end{itemize}

\item Example of potential uses of the data from the EBA transparency exercises in academic research
\item Very few papers  using quantil regression methodology for panel data in NPL literature
%\item Provide a first estimation of the impact of geographic diversification on NPLs
% \item Paper also provides interesting insights with potential practical implications for policy making, recommendations on NPL strategies, the design of Early Warning Indicators (EWI) and bank-stress testing methodology.
 \item Paper also provides interesting insights for policy making, the design of Early Warning Indicators (EWI), the preparation of recommendations on NPL strategies and bank-stress testing methodology.

%in The EU-banking sector.

% Implications for prudent bank stress testing, policy making, recommendations on NPL strategies and design of Early Warning Indicators (EWI),contributing to a more effective microprudential supervision of  banks in Europe.

\end{itemize}


}

